+# Keep sessions in process memory
+
+For whoever changes login or deploys the web app. Sessions now live in process memory because volunteers requested
+removing the Postgres read on every request to make the app faster.
+
+## Context
+
+In 2023 larder moved from one server to two behind a load balancer, and sessions stayed in each server's memory.
+Members were logged out whenever a request reached the other server, and every deploy logged out everyone; the pantry
+volunteers stopped deploying during opening hours. Postgres was chosen to preserve sessions across servers and
+deploys without adding a service. Volunteers have now requested a process-local dictionary to remove the database
+read on each request. This changes the previous requirement that a deploy log no one out; the speed improvement
+has not been measured.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Postgres**: the previous choice, shared by all servers and durable across deploys; replaced to remove the
+  database read on each request.
+- **Redis as a session store**: shared storage, but adds a service the volunteers would run only for sessions.
+- **Process memory**: chosen as requested, removing session database queries without adding a service.
+
+## Decision
+
+Use the dictionary-backed store in [`larder/session_store.py`](../../larder/session_store.py). Request handlers
+import its shared `sessions` instance so that requests in one process use the same store.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Session access needs no database query. See the [session storage limits](../features/sessions.md#known-limits)
+for the effects on deployment and routing. This checkout contains no request handlers or web app entry point, so
+integration into the running app remains outside this repository.
